> Then ReadOrderList $C310. Reached by OrderCommandJump $C339 with offset 0.
> Witness: L1 227 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C7E9 OrderListOp3 ; LD BC,nn: nn = the channel's order list (self-modified by UpdateTune)

$C7F7 c SetSlot ; Order command $E0+n: the next byte is the record offset of the channel's instrument slot n
> SlotTable[8 x channel + n] = the byte (SlotRowOp+1 gives 8 x channel, CommandParam $C1E0 n), through the self-modified SlotIndexOp. Then ReadOrderList $C310. Tune 0 uses it three times on channel B: slot 0 = $48 (record 8), slot 6 = $51 (record 9), then $5A (record 10); the change outlives the tune (StartTune does not reset SlotTable).
> Witness: L1 21 (explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).
$C807 OrderListOp4 ; LD BC,nn: nn = the channel's order list (self-modified by UpdateTune)
$C80F SlotIndexOp ; LD L,n: n = 8 x channel + n (self-modified at $C7FF)

$C81B c SetRepeat ; Order command $A0+n: play the next pattern n times
> The repeats left (+$04) = n - 1 (n 0: 255, so 256 plays); ReadPatternEvent $C2F6 replays the pattern while it is not 0. Then ReadOrderList $C310.
> Witness: L1 123 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).

$C825 c FetchPatternByte ; A = the byte at the channel's pattern position: DE + (+$02)
> Called by PatternEvent $C36D for a rest's duration and a slide's three bytes.
> Witness: L1 76 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).

@module tunes ; The tune data and the player's tables: periods, vibratos, arpeggios, tunes, order lists, patterns, envelope rates
@about The tune format, for a converter (all of it in tunes, music/tunes, $C967-$CD35): TuneTable $C967 holds 4 entries of 3 little-endian words, each the address of a channel's order list (A, B, C). An order list is bytes read one after another: $00-$7F pattern n; $80 t transpose (t signed); $A0+n the next pattern n times (0: 256); $C0 d detune d; $E0+n m instrument slot n = record offset m (9 x record); $FF the tune's end (the first channel to meet it ends the tune; the next frame tune 0 starts again). A pattern, found through PatternLo $CA37 / PatternHi $CA52, is events ended by $FF: n d (n $00-$7F) a note of d frames; $A0 d a rest of d frames; $80+n instrument slot n; $90+n arpeggio n (0 off); $BF slide off; $C0+s lo hi c a slide (bit 5 of the command: period up). Note n sounds at period-table index n + 20 + the channel's transpose (MIDI n + 32 + transpose). A step of the text format is one event; the time of a note or rest is its d frames (0 = 256), every other event takes none.
@about The instruments (Instruments $C028, music/instruments), the instrument slots (SlotTable $C11B, the state module), the vibratos (VibratoTable $C906), the arpeggios (ArpeggioTable $C927) and the envelope rates (AttackRates $CD36, DecayRates $CD46) belong to the tune too: a converter needs them to give each note its volume curve. The periods (PeriodLo $C82E, PeriodHi $C89A, music/periods.csv) are the player's equal-tempered table.
@about Tune 0 (3 min 14 s): channel A plays the bass (patterns 0, 3, then pattern 5 transposed through -7, -12, -9, -7, -4, -6, then pattern 10, 15), channel B the same intro detuned by 10, then the melody (pattern 6), then the chords (13, 16, 18, 19 with arpeggios 2-6 and the noise beats of instrument 8), channel C the noise rhythm (pattern 2), the arpeggiated line (7-9, transposed +12 / +14), then 11, 14, 17, 20. Tune 1 is tune 0 from pattern 10 on.

$C82D b EmptyPattern 1 ; A pattern of no events: every channel's pattern at a tune's start
> StartTune $C79F points PatternPointers $C1DA at it, so a channel's first frame reads $FF and goes to its order list. Witness: read by every recording that starts a tune (c1, explorers, the rzx pieces with an EndTune).

$C82E b PeriodLo 12 ; The tone periods' low bytes: 108 notes, C0 to B8
> Index i (StartNote $C4C9: the note + 20 + the transpose; ArpeggioEffect adds the step's semitones) is MIDI note i + 12 at the AY clock of 1.7734 MHz: index 57 = 252 = A4 (439.8 Hz). The period is PeriodLo[i] + 256 x PeriodHi[i]. Two flaws: every C# (indices 1, 13, 25, ..., 85) is 0.38 semitone flat, and index 8 (G#0, 4526) is G0's period again. Tune 0 plays C# at indices 25 and 61 (7 notes). The recordings read indices 14-96.
@res music/period-lo.bin
$C82E ; octave 0 (indices 0-11)
$C83A ; octave 1
$C846 ; octave 2
$C852 ; octave 3
$C85E ; octave 4
$C86A ; octave 5
$C876 ; octave 6
$C882 ; octave 7
$C88E ; octave 8

$C89A b PeriodHi 12 ; The tone periods' high bytes: 108 notes
> As PeriodLo. Its last 8 bytes ($C8FE-$C905, all 0) are also VibratoTable-8's record 0, which no instrument names (offset 0 is no vibrato).
@res music/period-hi.bin
$C8A6 ; octave 1
$C8B2 ; octave 2
$C8BE ; octave 3
$C8CA ; octave 4
$C8D6 ; octave 5
$C8E2 ; octave 6
$C8EE ; octave 7
$C8FA ; octave 8

$C906 b VibratoTable 8 ; The vibrato record at offset 8: a delay of 40 frames, the deltas +1, 0, -1, 0, +1, 0, -1
> Addressed as VibratoTable-8 + the instrument's +7 (StartNote $C51E, VibratoStep $C631): byte 0 the delay (bit 7 clear) or cycles (bit 7 set), bytes 1-7 the signed period deltas of one half cycle. Instruments 4, 5 and 7 name it. Witness: the recordings read all 8 bytes.
@res music/vibratos.bin

$C90E b VibratoSpare 8 ; 25 bytes after the vibrato record that nothing reads
> No instrument's +7 points here and no recording reads them; as vibrato records their deltas would be 18 to 128 period units. Known only from the code: left over.

$C927 b ArpeggioTable 8 ; The arpeggios: 8 records of 8 bytes, record n at ArpeggioTable + 8n (n 1-7; 0 is off)
> A record: a header (bit 7: loop; bits 3-6: the hold of the base note at the note's start, + 1; bits 0-2: the number of steps), then the steps (bits 3-7: the semitones over the note, bits 0-2: the hold in frames, 0 = 256). Read by StartNote $C52A and ArpeggioEffect $C694. Witness: the recordings read records 1-6 (the header and the steps).
@res music/arpeggios.bin
$C927 ; record 0: not used (an arpeggio command with nibble 0 is "off"); not read in any recording
$C92F ; record 1: once, first hold 6, steps +12 for 5 (patterns 7-9: an octave up from the 6th frame after the note's start)
$C937 ; record 2: loop, first hold 2, steps +3 for 1, +7 for 1 (a minor chord: patterns 13, 16, 18, 19)
$C93F ; record 3: loop, first hold 2, steps +4 for 1, +7 for 1 (a major chord: patterns 2, 13, 16)
$C947 ; record 4: loop, first hold 2, steps +5 for 1, +9 for 1 (patterns 13, 16)
$C94F ; record 5: loop, first hold 2, steps +3 for 1, +8 for 1 (patterns 13, 16)
