$CD36 b AttackRates 16 ; The attack rates: an instrument's +3 high nibble 1-15 (0 is the default, 15 a frame)
> Below $80: that many volume steps every frame; $80 + n: one step every n frames. Read by SetInstrument $C444. Witness: the recordings read entry 7 (4 a frame, instruments 4 and 7).
@res music/envelope-rates.bin

$CD46 b DecayRates 16 ; The decay and release rates: an instrument's +3 and +4 low nibbles 1-15
> As AttackRates; read by SetInstrument $C477 (decay) and $C4A0 (release). Witness: the recordings read entries 6-10, 12 and 13.
@res music/envelope-rates.bin

@module ay
$CD56 c AYWrite ; Write A to AY register D
> In: D = the register, A = the value, H = $FF, L = $BF, C = $FD. OUT ($FFFD),D selects the register, OUT ($BFFD),A writes it; B is left $BF. Called by WriteAY $C290 10 times a frame (7 for a tune other than 0).
> The recordings' only AY writer after the 128K ROM's power-on set-up (ports_out $FFFD, $BFFD in all 34). explorers: 134 110 calls in 13 411 writes (10 each).
> Witness: L1 739060 (c1, explorers, gameover, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx, rzx).

@module tunes
$CD5D b MusicLeftover 16 ; The rest of the block: 1187 bytes the player never reads
> Known only from the code: no instruction reads $CD5D-$D1FF and no recording reads it in RAM 6; the copy brings it along with the player ($6D5D-$71FF on the tape). Bit patterns, not tune data (no pointer leads here).
